./LOGBOOK.md:8:### 2011 — libssh2 adapter bounds cancellation-ignoring injected work (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:10:- REWORK: Timeout races now detach callers promptly while a bounded registry retains, cancels, and reports unfinished injected work until it actually returns. Upload keeps the libssh2 channel state machine actor-owned and applies one absolute deadline across open, source read, bounded drains, writes, EOF, and exit; only the injected source read is detached. External signer work follows the same visible ownership rule instead of making teardown wait indefinitely.
./LOGBOOK.md:12:- VERIFICATION: `swift test --filter LibSSH2` passes 37 tests/3 suites; `make validate-core` passes 371 tests/31 suites plus build and boundary checks; `make validate-libssh2`, source tamper gates, and the dual-architecture extension-safe macOS provider/harness matrix exit 0.
./LOGBOOK.md:14:### 1943 — libssh2 adapter closes review round 5 fatal teardown and ownership gaps (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:15:- ROOT CAUSE: Fatal socket failures were mapped inconsistently and could leave an apparently usable transport; automatic keepalive used its reply deadline while waiting behind KEX; teardown queues and task snapshots could grow or report optimistic zeros; and one-shot channel cleanup could discard a live libssh2 pointer after `EAGAIN`.
./LOGBOOK.md:16:- REWORK: One privacy-safe failure path now tears down every lane-fatal socket error. Automatic keepalive defers behind an active rekey and starts a fresh reply deadline after gate admission. Session/channel admission is capped, shared drains and teardown use single-flight tasks, and teardown cancels and joins all owned work before clearing handles. Channel close/free remains tracked until libssh2 confirms disposal; cleanup failure retains ownership for session teardown.
./LOGBOOK.md:17:- NONBLOCKING CLEANUP: A terminal socket enters an abort-drain mode that feeds EOF to receives and consumes only libssh2's already-retained outbound bytes. This lets the engine complete its nonblocking destructors without network I/O or fabricated operation success. Session destruction is bounded; failure remains explicit and keeps ownership visible rather than claiming a closed baseline.
./LOGBOOK.md:18:- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic regressions cover socket failure during open/read/write/keepalive, keepalive delayed beyond its ordinary reply timeout by KEX, exec-start rejection plus repeated free `EAGAIN`, concurrent close pressure, and the 64-operation channel cap. Deferred receive credit, RFC open reasons, exact exec-exit metadata, and deep rekey/keepalive observability remain explicit unsupported states owned by `TASK-260728-3cveay`.
./LOGBOOK.md:19:- VERIFICATION: `swift test --filter LibSSH2` passes 35 tests/3 suites; `make validate-core` passes 369 tests/31 suites plus build and boundary checks; `make validate-libssh2`, source tamper gates, and the dual-architecture extension-safe macOS provider/harness matrix exit 0. Strict recursive Swift format and diff checks pass. Logs use task-scoped `*-06.log` or later names under `.temp/TASK-260715-1ozsb6/`.
./LOGBOOK.md:21:### 1858 — libssh2 adapter closes review round 4 cancellation and cleanup gaps (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:22:- ROOT CAUSE: Long-lived reads reused the write-credit deadline; the authentication timeout raced a waiter that still joined an independently owned signer; rekey and keepalive deadlines began only after the shared session gate; and canceled upload cleanup inherited task cancellation, causing its cleanup progress handler to close the shared TCP bridge.
./LOGBOOK.md:23:- REWORK: Reads now wait without an implicit idle deadline while retaining operation-scoped cancellation. Socket and signer completion waiters let callers detach promptly, while teardown cancels and joins owned work. Rekey uses the earlier of the caller-visible explicit deadline and `SSHRekeyPolicy.timeout` before gate admission; one keepalive may queue behind KEX. EOF takes the per-channel write gate after accepted writes, and uncertain EOF/exec/upload failures run bounded uncancelled channel reset while siblings survive.
./LOGBOOK.md:24:- ERROR SHAPE: Engine channel-open rejection is operation-scoped with explicit `unsupported` RFC reason evidence. EOF and exec-exit failures carry channel scope; upload timeout carries operation scope; connection errors remain lane-scoped and fatal. No deferred semantic is fabricated.
./LOGBOOK.md:25:- VERIFICATION: `swift test --filter LibSSH2` passes 27 tests/3 suites; `make validate-core` passes 361 tests/31 suites plus build and boundary checks; strict Swift format and diff checks pass; `make validate-libssh2`, source tamper gates, and the dual-architecture extension-safe macOS provider/harness matrix all exit 0. Logs use the task-scoped `*-05.log` names under `.temp/TASK-260715-1ozsb6/`.
./LOGBOOK.md:27:### 1824 — libssh2 adapter preserves operation scope and nonblocking argument identity (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:28:- ROOT CAUSE: Channel read/write timeout cancellation shut down the shared socket bridge; channel opens rejected `rekeying` before entering the session gate and started their deadline only after gate admission; each rekey caller owned the connection-global KEX; and concurrent writes could resume libssh2's single per-channel write state with a different payload after `EAGAIN`. Credential results were checked against factory capabilities but not the caller-provided allowlist.
./LOGBOOK.md:29:- REWORK: Socket-service cancellation now retires only its independently cancellable readiness operation. Channel opens may queue behind rekey under their original bounded deadline. One bounded shared rekey flight coalesces trigger reasons and lets a canceled caller detach while KEX continues. Per-channel FIFO gates retain exact write arguments across `EAGAIN`, with queued bytes still reserved against the caller cap. Returned credential algorithms must belong to both adapter capabilities and the request allowlist.
./LOGBOOK.md:30:- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic loopback regressions prove canceled and timed-out reads leave sibling channels and the session ready; an open times out while rekey is deliberately suspended and a second queued open succeeds afterward; two rekey triggers produce one KEX while the first caller cancels; four concurrent same-channel payloads survive forced bridge backpressure byte-exactly; and a supported-but-disallowed credential algorithm fails before authentication.
./LOGBOOK.md:31:- VERIFICATION: `swift test --filter LibSSH2` passes 23 tests/3 suites; strict Swift format and `git diff --check` pass; `make validate-core` passes 357 tests/31 suites plus build and boundary checks; `make validate-libssh2`, `make test-libssh2-source-gates`, and `make native-apple-matrix` all exit 0. The four M3 semantics remain explicit `unsupported` states owned by `TASK-260728-3cveay`.
./LOGBOOK.md:33:### 1758 — libssh2 adapter loopback conformance closes review round 2 (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:34:- ROOT CAUSE: libssh2 keeps channel-open and reply-requiring global-request nonblocking state on the shared session, so actor reentrancy across `EAGAIN` allowed a second open to resume the first open's state. The custom send/receive callback ABI also requires negative socket errors directly; returning `-1` plus `errno=EAGAIN` made ordinary backpressure appear as a disconnect.
./LOGBOOK.md:35:- REWORK: A bounded FIFO session-operation gate now holds one permit across every channel-open, client-rekey, and keepalive/global-request `EAGAIN` sequence while independent channel payload I/O remains interleavable. Callback would-block paths return `-EAGAIN`. Timeout adaptation uses structured tasks and joins the losing branch; bridge shutdown marks terminal state, closes the injected connection, cancels and joins every queued service task, and only then discards buffers or frees engine state.
./LOGBOOK.md:36:- CONFORMANCE: A serialized macOS loopback fixture starts ephemeral OpenSSH with an in-memory P-256 external signer and proves host-policy-before-credential ordering, approved public-key authentication, direct-tcpip echo, concurrent exec opens, exec/stdin upload, server-rekey traffic survival, manual client rekey, keepalive transmission, five connected read/cancel races, and three successful connect/close resource baselines. The four M3 semantics remain explicit `unsupported` reports owned by `TASK-260728-3cveay`.
./LOGBOOK.md:37:- VERIFICATION: Strict recursive `swift-format` and `git diff --check`, `make validate-core` (353 tests/31 suites), `make validate-libssh2`, `make test-libssh2-source-gates`, and `make native-apple-matrix` all exit 0. `task-board validate` exits 0 and reports only the known story aggregate mismatch while this child is in development; no status or unsupported semantic is bypassed.
./LOGBOOK.md:39:### 1454 — libssh2 adapter review rework closes async ownership gaps (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:40:- ROOT CAUSE: Actor isolation serialized synchronous libssh2 calls but not the socket service work awaited between `LIBSSH2_ERROR_EAGAIN` retries. Reentrant progress calls could snapshot the same outbound prefix, issue duplicate writes, and later remove inconsistent byte counts; suspended operations also retained channel/session pointers while teardown could free them.
./LOGBOOK.md:42:- REWORK: Upload has an overall deadline and rejects oversized source chunks; timeout racing returns without waiting for a non-cooperative injected dependency. External signer work is explicitly tracked, notification-driven rather than 1 ms polled, cancelled, and joined. Connect failures retain their pre-teardown phase, timeout/cancellation counters reconcile, and automatic keepalive failures consume the configured miss budget before fatal teardown.
./LOGBOOK.md:43:- VERIFICATION: Focused adapter coverage grew from 7 to 11 tests, adding forced overlapping socket writes (exact bytes, maximum concurrency one), joined signer cancellation, resolution timeout phase/counter evidence, and 20 repeated connect-cancel resource baselines. Strict format/diff checks, `make validate-core` (349 tests/30 suites), `make validate-libssh2`, source tamper gates, and the dual-architecture extension-safe Apple matrix all exit 0.
./LOGBOOK.md:46:### 0351 — Pinned libssh2 adapter joins the macOS provider and harness graphs (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:47:- IMPLEMENTATION: `ReluxTunnelLibSSH2Adapter` is the sole Swift target importing the pinned static libssh2/OpenSSL XCFramework. A serialized transport actor drives custom send/receive callbacks in nonblocking mode over the injected `SSHTCPConnection`; bounded async bridges turn socket and external-Keychain-signer progress into `LIBSSH2_ERROR_EAGAIN` without blocking a provider executor.
./LOGBOOK.md:49:- DECISION: Consumer-driven receive credit, RFC channel-open reasons, exact exec-exit presence/`coreDumped`, and deep server-KEX/keepalive reporting are all emitted as `unsupported`, owned by `TASK-260728-3cveay`. Client rekey, server-rekey protocol handling, bounded channel I/O, exec/stdin upload, and forced `keepalive@openssh.com` transmission remain implemented M0 behavior; no unavailable value is inferred from libssh2 internals.
./LOGBOOK.md:50:- ANOMALY: Xcode's generated package scheme copied both library-style XCFramework module maps to one custom `Release/include/module.modulemap`, producing a deterministic multiple-producer error once the libssh2 and native-fixture graphs met. The Apple matrix now keeps Xcode's existing device/simulator/native checks and uses isolated dual-architecture SwiftPM builds with `-application-extension` for the macOS provider/harness; the stripped release harness check verifies libssh2's intentionally hidden local rekey symbol separately from exported HEV symbols.
./LOGBOOK.md:51:- VERIFICATION: Strict recursive Swift format lint, `make validate-core` (345 tests/30 suites), `make validate-libssh2`, pinned-source tamper gates, and the revised Apple matrix all exit 0. Focused adapter tests cover bounded partial transport I/O, oversized-read rejection, async signer EAGAIN, explicit deferred capability states, and 20 repeated failed handshakes returning sessions/channels/sockets/tasks/custom allocations/buffers to zero.
./LOGBOOK.md:54:- DECISION: ADR-023 is now exhaustive in `.spec/ssh-transport-conformance-contract.md` and `SSHConformanceRequirement`: 16 operational/security requirements are `m0ViabilityMandatory`; consumer-driven receive credit/cap, RFC channel-open rejection reasons, exact exec-exit presence/`coreDumped`, and deep server-KEX/keepalive observability are `m3Deferred(ownerTaskID: "TASK-260728-3cveay")`.
./LOGBOOK.md:55:- IMPLEMENTATION: `SSHDeferredSemanticReport` exposes `reported`, `notReported`, and `unsupported`. Exact receive-credit policy/snapshots, open reasons, exit metadata, KEX generation, window/server-rekey/keepalive metrics, and manual keepalive RTT now require one of those states; no default zero/false or mandatory full-fidelity policy remains. Host-before-auth, approved public-key auth, direct-tcpip, exec/stdin upload, client and server rekey handling, bounded buffers/backpressure, cancellation/lifecycle, Keychain-only credential references, keepalive transmission, available metrics, and privacy-safe errors remain mandatory.
./LOGBOOK.md:58:- EVIDENCE: The revised contract preserves and cites all three libssh2 stop-line packets, the ReluxNIOSSH adapter/window packet, the candidate audit, exact source locations, and accepted header/patch SHA-256 values. The original contract outcome and the preconditions for `TASK-260715-1ozsb6`, `TASK-260715-2d3g5e`, `TASK-260715-1u2vpc`, and `TASK-260728-3cveay` now carry the same revision.
./LOGBOOK.md:59:- VERIFICATION: Focused SSH contract suite passes 13 tests; strict Swift format, diff checks, core boundary guard, native/libssh2 verification, full 336-test/29-suite `swift test`, and `swift build` pass. `make validate-core` exits 0.
./LOGBOOK.md:273:- ANOMALY: Round 2 removed `1u2vpc ← 2ayxqn`, which scheduled the libssh2 functional/rekey matrix **before** Gate P0 existed while the matrix scope still required a Gate-P0 provider smoke on this Mac. Restored rather than re-scoped, so no Apple-target row is weakened. Consequence: M0 engine selection (`1gjxer`) correctly lands after P0.
./LOGBOOK.md:293:- DECISION: Owner-approved strategy encoded in `.spec/decisions.md` as ADR-023 (M0 SSH viability scope, Option A, with the four deferred semantics named and owned by TASK-260728-3cveay) and ADR-024 (macOS-only physical target, iOS deferred, Linux CI nonblocking, Relux Works signing/notary available). ADR-013 re-scoped from a blocking prototype gate to a release-path gate; ADR-014 moved Open → Accepted with libssh2 as the primary engine.
./LOGBOOK.md:516:- DECISION: `TCPAdmissionRegistry` is one generation-scoped mutex authority for pending handshakes, authenticated flows, concurrent channel opens, owned channels, fixed directional queue capacity, live buffered bytes, and phase counts. All ceilings and the measured-safe/HEV evidence gates are caller-injected; denial is synchronous, retains no waiter, maps to SOCKS5 `REP=01`, and explicitly prohibits an SSH open. Reservation identifiers are monotonically allocated without reuse and fail closed with finite `identifier_capacity` pressure before `UInt64` wrap.
./LOGBOOK.md:518:- DIAGNOSTICS: Runtime schema v1 is additively extended to 139 fixed counters, 75 fixed gauges, and two 12-bucket cumulative histograms. Registry-owned current gauges publish through a generation-scoped fixed-size atomic coalescer and reconcile into snapshots independently of the bounded event lane, so dropped counter/histogram ingestion remains observable without leaving phantom flow/open/channel/byte gauges. The TCP surface contains only aggregate pressure/terminal tokens, counts, bytes, health, and channel-open latency; it accepts no destination, hostname, address, port, payload, credential, flow/channel ID, or free-form label. Maximum populated encoding is 13,568 bytes of the 65,536-byte limit and generation advancement isolates stale publications.
./LOGBOOK.md:747:- MILESTONE: BUG-260720-2zh86a accepted → done. `make validate-native` full Apple matrix no longer stops on the HEV/libssh2 deployment-target mismatch; packet-plane Apple builds and physical validation (12x6oq) unblocked on this axis.
./LOGBOOK.md:748:- VERIFICATION: Reviewer reran independently on the exact tree: `make validate-native` exit 0 (iOS device/sim + macOS provider/harness matrix, 110 Swift tests), `make validate-core` exit 0, `make validate-libssh2` real rekey/KEX exit 0. `otool -l` confirmed every slice: HEV ios/tvos minos 18.0, macos 15.0; ReluxLibSSH2 ios 18.0, macos 15.0 — no 10.14/11.0 records remain.
./LOGBOOK.md:756:- VERIFICATION: `make validate-native` passed the complete iOS device, iOS Simulator, macOS provider/harness and stripped-link audit matrix, followed by 110 Swift tests and `swift build`. `make validate-core`, `make validate-libssh2` (real rekey/KEX/global-request test), native negative gates, strict Swift format, Python compile, shell syntax, JSON parsing, and `git diff --check` passed.
./LOGBOOK.md:806:### 1208 — libssh2 adapter hits third-round channel/exec/window API boundary (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:808:- BLOCKER: `ssh2_channel_open` parses all RFC 4254 rejection reasons, then collapses them to `LIBSSH2_ERROR_CHANNEL_FAILURE`; no public typed/numeric reason survives. The remaining diagnostic string is not a stable control-flow API.
./LOGBOOK.md:810:- ROUTING: This is the third fork-gap round anticipated by TASK-260720-2sltje. Do not add a partial/mock-only adapter. Recommend failing libssh2 red at M0 unless its value justifies one explicitly approved consolidated fork extension for manual windows, typed open failures, and lossless exec-exit metadata; the alternative is an explicit M0-to-M3/common-contract revision. Exact evidence and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_third-public-api-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:812:### 1200 — Bounded libssh2 observation fork clears the remaining public-API clauses (TASK-260720-2sltje)
./LOGBOOK.md:813:- DECISION: The existing libssh2 patch remains a single allowlisted six-file delta. It adds typed server-initiated KEX `STARTED`/`SUCCEEDED`/`FAILED` observation with a monotonic generation and one bounded, nonblocking reply-correlated global-request operation; packet ordering, encryption, MAC, KEX algorithms, and crypto implementations remain unchanged.
./LOGBOOK.md:814:- DECISION: A timed-out global request stays outstanding until its late reply is drained. This deliberately prevents an unnumbered SSH `REQUEST_SUCCESS`/`REQUEST_FAILURE` from being miscorrelated with a newer request; callers can record a miss at `LIBSSH2_ERROR_TIMEOUT` and continue the same operation to restore the request slot.
./LOGBOOK.md:816:- ANOMALY: The broader pre-existing HEV Apple matrix stops before any Xcode build because `HevSocks5Tunnel.xcframework` contains macOS 10.14 and 11.0 object minima while its manifest requires only 10.14. This artifact is outside the libssh2 delta; the task-specific XCFramework inspection and required `make validate-core` gates pass.
./LOGBOOK.md:818:### 1145 — libssh2 adapter remains blocked on server-rekey observability and keepalive replies (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:819:- BLOCKER: The accepted `libssh2_session_rekey` fork clears client byte/time/manual trigger initiation only. The exact public header exports no KEX lifecycle callback or generation query. Pinned libssh2 handles inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` entirely inside private `packet.c`/`transport.c` state, so the adapter cannot truthfully emit `.serverInitiated`, mark `activeKeyExchange`, increment generation, or distinguish server-KEX success/failure through the public boundary.
./LOGBOOK.md:820:- BLOCKER: `libssh2_keepalive_send` writes `keepalive@libssh2.org` with `want_reply=1` but returns after queueing and stores no outstanding request or reply outcome. The public API exposes no generic reply-requiring global request or request-success/failure correlation. Consequently the adapter cannot measure reply RTT, enforce reply timeout/miss allowance, or distinguish acknowledgement from silence.
./LOGBOOK.md:821:- EVIDENCE: The public macOS XCFramework exports only `libssh2_session_rekey`, `libssh2_keepalive_config`, and `libssh2_keepalive_send` among relevant symbols. `keepalive.c:52-96` contains no reply wait/state; inbound server rekey is private at `packet.c:1336-1369` and `transport.c:407-425,1039-1048`. Trace callbacks are unavailable in non-debug builds and are not a supported protocol API.
./LOGBOOK.md:822:- ROUTING: Do not report these gates green or build a mock-only partial adapter. Recommend a separately reviewed minimal fork extension exposing typed server-KEX lifecycle plus a nonblocking reply-correlated global-request/keepalive API; alternatives are explicitly failing the libssh2 candidate or revising the mandatory contract. Exact options and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_server-rekey-keepalive-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:824:### 1115 — Minimal libssh2 public client-rekey fork clears the adapter blocker (TASK-260720-3vwls7)
./LOGBOOK.md:825:- DECISION: Pinned libssh2 `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` carries one allowlisted two-file patch: `include/libssh2.h` publicly declares `libssh2_session_rekey`, and `src/session.c` adds a null-checked, nonblocking-friendly `BLOCK_ADJUST` wrapper over the existing `ssh2_kex_exchange(session, 1, &session->startup_key_state)`. No crypto, algorithm, packet, channel, or private-header surface changed.
./LOGBOOK.md:826:- SUPPLY CHAIN: The builder verifies libssh2 and OpenSSL 3.5.7 archive SHA-256 values before extraction or patch application, checks source-license hashes, rebuilds five static Apple architecture slices, emits the combined BSD-3-Clause/Apache-2.0/acknowledgement notice, and fails closed on patch/hash/path drift. Two clean rebuilds produced byte-identical XCFramework artifact locks.
./LOGBOOK.md:827:- VERIFICATION: The public-header-only C integration test drove the wrapper through three `EAGAIN` results, observed client-initiated NEWKEYS installation, then forced OpenSSH `RekeyLimit` server-initiated NEWKEYS and proved post-rekey channels remained usable. Fork verify/rekey/source-tamper gates, Swift public-module packaging test, native seam tests, strict Swift format, 62 core tests, and `swift build` pass; `ReluxTunnelCore` remains free of libssh2/OpenSSL imports.
./LOGBOOK.md:829:### 1030 — libssh2 adapter stops on absent public client-rekey API (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:830:- BLOCKER: Exact pinned archive `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` reproduced SHA-256 `744ba3e9…c87c`. Public `include/libssh2.h` has no rekey operation; initial `libssh2_session_handshake` calls private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=0)`, while only inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` reaches private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=1)` from `src/packet.c`.
./LOGBOOK.md:831:- IMPACT: Mandatory byte/time/explicit client rekey and `SSHTransport.requestRekey` cannot be implemented through the pinned public API. Calling the private symbol, exporting it in-task, replaying handshake, or reporting success without KEX would violate the task's no-patch/no-hidden-fallback boundary. Product code and partial packaging stopped before implementation.
./LOGBOOK.md:832:- ROUTING: Recommend recording libssh2 red/failed at this pin. The alternative is a separately authorized, audited libssh2 fork/public rekey hook (or a future upstream pin followed by full re-audit). Exact evidence and tradeoffs are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_libssh2-rekey-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:843:- FINDING: Snapshot lifecycle is sticky-correct: `SSHConnectionStateMachine`'s own snapshot resets to nil on transition to `userAuthentication`/`active`, but `NIOSSHHandler` re-reads it after every processed message while the KEX state machine still holds `NegotiationResult`, and only overwrites on non-nil (`NIOSSHHandler.swift:684`). Consequence: during a rekey the handler snapshot updates when the new `NegotiationResult` is computed (KEXINIT processing), slightly before newKeys activate.
./LOGBOOK.md:844:- NOTE: Adapter caveats: (1) for AEAD ciphers (`aes*-gcm@openssh.com`) `macAlgorithm` reports the negotiated-but-unused MAC-list value — standard SSH semantics, do not treat it as the wire MAC; (2) `.global` promise resolves `.failure` as success-with-value (RTT measurable on refusal — correct for `keepalive@openssh.com` liveness), only connection errors fail the promise; (3) untested-but-documented paths: `.success` reply payload for `.global` requests and empty-allowlist fail-closed negotiation — acceptable residual risk, worth covering when the adapter integrates.
./LOGBOOK.md:848:- IMPLEMENTATION: Bounded generic global requests always set `want-reply`, use the existing protected packet queue, and resolve ordered success/failure replies. `keepalive@openssh.com` is proven by an explicit failure reply (valid liveness), with no TCP-forwarding listener or child channel.
./LOGBOOK.md:855:- BLOCKER: ReluxNIOSSH exposes only TCP-forwarding as a public global request. Its generic reply-requiring request sender and message type are internal, so the adapter cannot send/measure a supported `keepalive@openssh.com` request. Substituting remote TCP forwarding or writing encrypted packets beside the engine is invalid.
./LOGBOOK.md:857:- ROUTING: Product code stopped per TASK-260715-1af33i's explicit insufficient-fork rule. Recommended resume path is a focused TASK-260715-nzdzv3 fork extension for async external signing, generic/specific keepalive reply observation, configurable algorithm allowlists, and an immutable negotiated-algorithm snapshot/event. Full evidence and rejected forced fits are attached as `TASK-260715-1af33i_fork-api-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:872:- FINDING: During any KEX (client- or server-initiated) upstream's state machine rejects outbound `channelData` with `protocolViolation` (`SSHConnectionStateMachine.swift:1060-1076`), failing the child write promise. Pre-existing upstream behavior, unchanged by the fork — but automatic byte/time rekey makes the collision window likely under bulk traffic. The NIOSSH adapter (TASK-260715-1af33i) MUST gate/queue child writes on `rekeySnapshot.keyExchangeInProgress` / `NIOSSHRekeyStartedEvent`→`NIOSSHRekeySucceededEvent`; the fork exposes exactly this observability for the contract's "writes MAY suspend" clause.
./LOGBOOK.md:874:- NOTE: `.serverInitiated` rekey reason semantically means "remote-initiated" (a server-role handler labels client-triggered KEX the same way); cosmetic for the client-only tunnel. `requestRekey` in a non-active state fails the promise and also fires `errorCaught` — callers should not treat pre-active requests as recoverable no-ops.
./LOGBOOK.md:876:### 0850 — Minimal ReluxNIOSSH window/rekey fork is reviewable (TASK-260715-nzdzv3)
./LOGBOOK.md:877:- DECISION: The source fork is an independent SwiftPM package at `Dependencies/ReluxNIOSSH`, based byte-for-byte on SwiftNIO SSH `0.14.1` / `31cdc3c` before a 16-file allowlisted delta. The `NIOSSH` product/module and upstream 16 MiB/no-auto-rekey defaults remain source-compatible; `ReluxTunnelCore` has no dependency on the fork.
./LOGBOOK.md:878:- IMPLEMENTATION: Child initial window/cap/adjustment policy is set through a normal pre-open channel option and observed through snapshots/events; returned credit is earned only after pipeline delivery. Client manual, per-direction protected-byte, and injected-monotonic-time rekeys share the existing KEX state machine, coalesce concurrent reasons/promises, reset generation counters on success, and observe server-initiated KEX without product reflection or a test-only API.
./LOGBOOK.md:882:- DECISION: Both engine adapters use one serialized, type-erased `ReluxTunnelCore` boundary with mandatory raw host-key policy evaluation before credential access, bounded partial writes, independent byte-stream halves, capped observable per-channel receive credit, automatic byte/time plus explicit/server rekey, keepalive, stable errors, schema-v1 metrics/events, and privacy-safe opaque lane identity. ADR-014 remains open; unsupported mandatory behavior is a red matrix row, never a candidate-specific skip.
./LOGBOOK.md:884:- VERIFICATION: The contract defines every operation, state, timeout/cancellation scope, error, metric, privacy rule, and E-ALGO/E-HOSTAUTH/E-CHANNELS/E-BACKPRESSURE/E-WINDOW/E-REKEY/E-KEEPALIVE/E-CANCEL/E-SCALE/E-ERRORS/E-METRICS-PRIVACY/E-INJECTION row. Three task-scoped PlantUML sources pass syntax checking and their lifecycle, pre-auth host verification, and channel/window/rekey SVG renders were visually inspected.
./LOGBOOK.md:887:- MILESTONE: Audit accepted → `done`. Every load-bearing claim reproduced from upstream: tag→commit pins for both candidates plus all six transitive deps (ls-remote), all three archive SHA-256 and both license hashes (fresh downloads), 16 MiB window `SSHChannelMultiplexer.swift:211`, internal `_rekey()` `NIOSSHHandler.swift:511`, libssh2 window APIs and direct-tcpip hardcode `src/channel.c:384,457`, unexported reexchange `src/packet.c:1369`, version-macro anomaly, six CVE fix commits confirmed ancestors of `a343024`, GHSA advisories via API, exactly 679 libssh2 commits/12mo.
./LOGBOOK.md:892:- SECURITY: SwiftNIO SSH is pinned to `0.14.1` / `31cdc3c`, which fixes critical CVE-2026-43798, with resolved Swift Crypto `4.5.1` and SwiftNIO `2.101.3` security-patched transitives. libssh2 release `1.11.1` is not a clean baseline because six advisories were fixed after release; the candidate is pinned to untagged `1.11.2_DEV` commit `a343024` containing all six fixes, with OpenSSL `3.5.7`. Re-audit on a signed libssh2 1.11.2+ release is mandatory.
./LOGBOOK.md:893:- FINDING: SwiftNIO SSH hardcodes each child receive-window target to 16 MiB and exposes client `_rekey()` only internally. libssh2 exposes generic per-channel initial/remaining/adjust APIs, but its direct-tcpip helper fixes the 2 MiB default and it likewise has no public client-rekey API or automatic byte/time threshold.
./LOGBOOK.md:894:- DECISION: ADR-014 remains open. Both candidates retain identical host-key, window, sustained 5 GiB rekey, scale (through 1,000 channels), cancellation, algorithm, extension-build, memory, and physical-size gates. Unknowns are named experiments in `.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md`; no missing capability was waived.
./LOGBOOK.md:895:- RISK: libssh2 master issue #2023 contains an unresolved AEAD correctness report whose specific leak interpretation is disputed. Treat it as an ASan/packet-content experiment, not a proven leak or a reason to prejudge the engine.
